.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 148 PM PST Tue Nov 17 2026
(This evening through Wednesday night)

Isolated to scattered rain showers continue out over the Pacific
this afternoon and are pushing inland. Otherwise, mostly sunny sky
conditions persist over the region with temperatures some 5 to 10
degrees below normal. Let`s get into the meat of the forecast!
Tonight, prefrontal light to moderate rain is expected across the
North Bay and coastal terrain along the San Francisco Peninsula.
Rain will increase in coverage and intensity around between 4 AM
and 8 AM Wednesday morning as the actual cold front arrives and
moves through the North Bay. As the front moves southeastward
through the morning, it will move through the Bay Area and Santa
Cruz Mountains between 6 AM and 10 AM, and then remainder of the
north Central Coast between late 10 AM and 4 PM. Again, light to
moderate rain is expected along the coastal ranges ahead of the
boundary given the strong southerly winds providing orographic
lift. There is also the potential for isolated thunderstorms as
MUCAPE values approach 250 J/kg along the frontal boundary.

Speaking of winds, a Wind Advisory will go into effect at 9 PM
this evening for coastal locations and the East Bay Interior
Valleys where S/SW between 20-30 MPH with gusts up to 50 MPH are
likely through 1 PM Wednesday. A High Wind Warning is also in
effect for the same time frame for the East Bay Hills where gusts
up to 70 MPH are expected, especially around the Altamont Pass.

Post-frontal rain showers and isolated thunderstorms are forecast to
persist Wednesday night and into Thursday morning as a colder air
mass moves into the region. This will also lead to increased
potential for thunderstorms with as a surface low pressure system
forecast to move into the Bay Area or Central Coast Thursday
morning. This is when midlevel lapse rates approach 6-8 deg C/km and
MUCAPE values between 300-600 J/kg.

Tonight through Friday morning, we can expect 1"-2" of rainfall in
the rain shadowed valleys, 1.5"-2.5" across the rest of the Bay Area
and Central Coast, 2"-3.5" in the North Bay, and 3"-5" in the Santa
Cruz Mountains and Santa Lucia Range.

