.BEACHES...
Issued at 148 PM PST Tue Nov 17 2026

For the Beach Hazards Statement/High Surf Advisory:

A Beach Hazards Statement remains in effect through 4 PM Wednesday
for an increased risk of sneaker waves, strong rip currents, and
breaking waves of 13 to 17 ft. A High Surf Advisory will then go
into effect from 4 PM Wednesday through 4 PM Thursday with breaking
waves of 15 to 18. The risk of sneaker waves and strong rip currents
will persist as well. These waves are less energetic than the ones
we saw on Saturday, which ended up flooding portions of Pacifica and
damaging Capitola Pier, but remain hazardous to potential swimmers,
could still knock unaware people into the water, and continue to
feed an increased risk of rip currents. The risk of drowning remains
high. Do not go in the water, avoid climbing on the rocks or leaning
over seawalls, and never turn your back on the ocean.

For the Coastal Flood Advisory:

A combination of high astronomical tides and thermal expansion from
warmer than normal water will result in tidal flooding concerns
across Bayshore locations in the San Francisco Bay and San Pablo
Bay. At the San Francisco tidal gauge, high tide is expected to be
1.3 ft above normal (7.1 ft MLLW) at 6:45 AM Wednesday and 1.2 ft
above normal (7.0 ft MLLW) at 7:15 AM Thursday. These predictions
include up to 1.3 ft of surge that will enhance the astronomical
tide and flooding threat. High tide varies up to 90 minutes earlier
or later along the Pacific Coast and through the San Francisco Bay,
respectively.

